\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Basic Interferometer on Point Sources, the Sun, and the Moon
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
By Patrick Kantorski
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
April 8, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This report was written for a radio astronomy undergraduate course at UC
 Berkeley.
 Radio interferometry is a powerful observational method used in Radio Astronomy
 for observing the sky in the Fourier domain.
 In this lab, my partners and I used a local radio interferometer at Berkeley
 to observe the Sun, the Moon, and point sources.
 We were also able to approximate the base line of our interferometer and
 measure the diameter of the Sun and the Moon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The purpose of this lab on interferometry was to demonstrate diffraction
 theory as it applies to a real radio interferometer.
 Fringes along with their amplitudes and phases were examined along with
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Fourier transform
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 relationship between the interferometer's response and luminosity of the
 sky.
 Additionally, my partners and I had to learn a lot about time, coordinates,
 and conversions to progress in this lab.
 We also obtained horizon-to-horizon data on various astronomical sources
 ranging from point sources to the Sun and Moon.
 When performing data analysis, we use linear and nonlinear least-squares
 fitting methods to filter out accurate point source positions and obtain
 approximate diameters for the Sun and Moon along with an estimate value
 of our two-element interferometer's baseline.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Background Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Astronomical Coordinates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are many different ways to go about keeping track of time and position
 using coordinate systems.
 For us to make and record accurate astronomical observations, we must know
 both where on Earth we are positioned and the current time relative to
 such location.
 By knowing both the position and time of observations, the direction from
 which a telescope is pointed can be accurately deciphered.
 Latitude and longitude coordinates work well to encode relative positions
 on the Earth.
 However because the Earth is not perfectly spherical, detailed surveys
 using latitude and longitude are not quite as simple as they may appear.
 On the contrary, accurately keeping track of time has its own issues.
 On Earth, clocks are tuned to different times according to which time zone
 and year a person is currently in.
 However in astronomy, using time zones to keep track of time is not particularl
y useful.
 To make counting time easier, astronomers use systems that do away with
 time zones and synchronize time to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

solar day
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at a position of 0° longitude, which also happens to conveniently run through
 the British Royal Observatory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /Users/ppkantorski/Documents/Radio_Astronomy/Lab_3/Pictures/sidereal_day.png
	scale 50
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
On Earth, because the planet experiences prograde motion, a sidereal day
 is slightly shorter than a solar day.
 At position 1, the Sun and a distant star behind are both directly overhead.
 At position 2, the Earth rotates about 360°, making the distant star behind
 the sun appear again directly overhead.
 This shift from position 1 to position 2 is called a sidereal day.
 However, by the time the Earth rotates back to face the Sun, as seen in
 position 3, the Sun is again directly overhead, but the distant star behind
 is not.
 Since the revolution around the Sun changes the angle from which the Sun
 is seen relative to the Earth, position 1 to position 3 shows how long
 it takes the Sun to return to its maximum height at precisely noon everyday.
 This shift from position 1 to position 3 is called a solar day.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Figure Sidereal"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A solar day is the amount of time it takes for the sun to return back to
 the same position on Earth.
 Another important thing to keep track of while trying to accurately track
 time is to count seconds.
 One might think that counting days is more practical, but in fact, days
 can be fairly complicated.
 Since the Earth is moving around the Sun while it is spinning around it's
 axis, the Earth must actually rotate a little more than 60°, or 2π radians,
 in order to return to the same position each day.
 Instead of spinning precisely 360° every 24 hours, or 86400s, it actually
 takes the Earth about 86164.0908s.
 This amount of time called a sidereal day and is slightly longer than a
 solar day (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "Figure Sidereal"

\end_inset

).
 For every day of the year except January 1, sidereal and solar days are
 out of sync.
 When trying to accurately track time, we really should start from January
 1.
 However, the question then becomes what time and year relative to January
 1 should one begin counting from?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In astronomy, astronomers typically count time in solar days starting from
 noon, January 1, 4713 BC, also known as the Julian date.
 On the other hand, computers count time in seconds from midnight at the
 start of January 1, 1970.
 This is called “Unix Time” and is pretty close to Coordinated Universal
 Time (UTC) which is derived directly from atomic clocks.
 Today, there have been a few revisions to UTC with several leap seconds
 added in here and there.
 Additionally, because the Earth's rotation is slowly slowing down, and
 keeping time can become extremely complicated.
 By using Network Timing Protocol (NTP) and/or the Global Positioning System
 (GPS), computers and cell phones routinely synchronize their clocks to
 atomic standards with milliseconds of precision.
 Because UTC is widely available to most people today, this makes it relatively
 easy for us Astronomers to accurately track time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Coordinate Conversion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Longitude and Latitude
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Longitude and latitude coordinates are considered geographic coordinates
 (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "Latitude & Longitude Figure"

\end_inset

).
 A geographic coordinate system is a coordinate system that enables any
 location on the Earth to be specified by a set of numbers or letters.
 The latitude of a point on the Earth's surface is the angle between the
 equatorial plane and the straight line that passes through that point normal
 to the surface.
 Lines joining points of the same latitude form circles on the surface of
 the Earth known as parallels because they are parallel to the equator and
 to each other.
 While the +90° parallel represents the north pole and -90° parallel represents
 the south pole, the 0° parallel of latitude is at the equator which divides
 the globe into Northern and Southern Hemispheres.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /Users/ppkantorski/Documents/Radio_Astronomy/Lab_3/Pictures/latitude_longitude.png
	scale 48
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
On Earth, latitude coordinates are measured as angles from the Equator to
 +90º (North Pole) to -90º (South Pole).
 Longitude coordinates are measured from the Prime Meridian from 0º to +180º
 due right and from 0 to -180º due left.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Latitude & Longitude Figure"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The longitude of a point on the Earth's surface is the angle east or west
 from the reference meridian to another meridian that passes through that
 point.
 The Prime Meridian is the line chosen as the international zero-longitude
 reference line and was set with the intention of passing through the British
 Royal Observatory in Greenwich, UK.
 Places to the east of the Prime Meridian are considered to be in the eastern
 hemisphere while places to the west are in the western hemisphere.
 Longitude also ranges from about -180º to +180º relative to the Prime Meridian.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, converting from rectangular latitude and longitude coordinates
 to spherical coordinates can be done using the vector matrix shown below
 in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Long / Lat Rotation"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{x}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\cos(lat)\cos(long)\\
\cos(lat)\sin(long)\\
\sin(lat)
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:Long / Lat Rotation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Right Ascension and Declination (
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\sigma$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In spherical coordinates, the position of stars are often expressed in right
 ascension and declination without use of a coordinate for distance.
 Because celestial objects are extremely far way, astronomers often have
 little to no information about their exact distances and therefore use
 only the object's direction.
 Additionally, the direction of fairly distant objects is the same for all
 observers and it is convenient to specify this direction with the same
 coordinates for every object.
 On the contrary, through the horizontal coordinate system, a star's position
 differs from observer to observer based on their positions on the Earth's
 surface and continuously changes with the Earth's rotation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /Users/ppkantorski/Documents/Radio_Astronomy/Lab_3/Pictures/ra_dec.png
	scale 46
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The figure above shows right ascension and declination as seen on the inside
 of the celestial sphere.
 The primary direction of the system is the vernal equinox or where the
 ascending node of the ecliptic in red meets the celestial equator in blue.
 Right ascension is measured eastward along the celestial equator from the
 primary direction while declination is measured due north or south from
 the equator.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "RA & Dec Figure"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equatorial coordinate system is a widely used celestial coordinate system
 used to specify the positions of celestial objects.
 Two coordinates typically used in the equatorial coordinate system are
 the right ascension and declination (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "RA & Dec Figure"

\end_inset

).
 Right ascension (symbol α, abbreviated RA) measures the angular distance
 of an object eastward along the celestial equator from the vernal equinox
 to the hour circle passing through the object.
 The vernal equinox point is one of the two points where the ecliptic intersects
 with the celestial equator.
 In comparison to longitude, right ascension is similarly but is measured
 in sidereal hours, minutes and seconds instead of degrees.
 This results from measuring right ascensions by timing the passage of objects
 across the meridian as the Earth rotates.
 In total, there are (360° / 24h) = 15° in one hour of right ascension and
 24h of right ascension around the entire celestial equator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The celestial equator is the projection of the Earth's equator onto the
 celestial sphere.
 Declination (symbol δ, abbreviated Dec) measures the angular distance of
 an object perpendicular to the celestial equator.
 Declination ranges from +180º northward to -180º to the south.
 The north celestial pole, for instance, has a declination of +90°.
 The origin of the coordinate of declination is the celestial equator itself.
 Declination can also be thought of as analogous to latitude.
 When the two coordinates are used together, right ascension and declination
 are typically abbreviated as (RA, Dec) or (
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\sigma$
\end_inset

).
 Because RA and Dec are an analogous to longitude and latitude, Equation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Long / Lat Rotation"

\end_inset

 can also be used to convert RA and Dec to spherical coordinates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Hour Angle and Declination (
\begin_inset Formula $ha,\sigma$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the equatorial coordinate system, right ascension can also be translated
 to hour angle (abbreviated HA or LHA).
 Hour angle positioning is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

left-handed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 system which measures the angular distance of an object westward along
 the celestial equator from the observer's meridian to the hour circle passing
 through the object (See Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "Hour Angle Figure"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /Users/ppkantorski/Documents/Radio_Astronomy/Lab_3/Pictures/hour_angle.png
	scale 46
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The figure above shows right ascension measured eastward along the celestial
 equator from the primary direction along with the hour angle abbreviated
 by LHA.
 The hour angle coordinate is the right ascension summed with the local
 sidereal time.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Hour Angle Figure"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike right ascension, hour angle increases with the Earth's rotation.
 Hour angle is used to measure the time from when an object crosses the
 meridian.
 For instance, a star on the observer's celestial meridian is said to have
 a zero hour angle.
 About one sidereal hour later, the Earth's rotation will carry the star
 to the west of the meridian and its hour angle becomes +1h.
 Right ascension may also be converted into hour angle as an intermediate
 step to converting to azimuth and altitude by multiplying the spherical
 coordinates with the rotational matrix shown below in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:RA to HA"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{(\alpha,\delta)\rightarrow(ha,\delta)}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\cos(LST) & \sin(LST) & 0\\
-\sin(LST) & \cos(LST) & 0\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:RA to HA}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Azimuth and Altitude (
\begin_inset Formula $az,alt)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The horizontal coordinate system is a celestial coordinate system that utilizes
 an observer's local horizon as it's fundamental plane.
 This coordinate system divides the sky into an upper hemisphere and a lower
 hemisphere.
 In the upper hemisphere, objects and stars are visible or within sight
 while in the lower hemisphere, the our view of the sky is blocked by the
 Earth.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

great circle
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 separating hemispheres is called celestial horizon or rational horizon
 and the pole of the upper hemisphere is called the zenith.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Altitude (abbreviated Alt) is sometimes referred to as elevation or the
 angle between the object and the observer's local horizon.
 For visible objects in the sky, altitude is an angle between 0º to 90º.
 Azimuth (abbreviated Az) is the angle of the object about the horizon and
 is usually measured from the north while increasing towards the east.
 Because the horizontal coordinate system is fixed to the Earth and not
 the stars, the altitude and azimuth of an object changes with time as the
 object appears to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

drift
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 across the sky.
 Furthermore, because the horizontal system is defined by the observer's
 position or local horizon, different locations on Earth viewing the same
 object at the same time will have different values of altitude and azimuth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By multiplying spherical hour angle and declination coordinates by the rotationa
l matrix shown below in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:HA, Dec to Az, Alt"

\end_inset

, we can convert (
\begin_inset Formula $ha,\sigma$
\end_inset

) coordinates to (
\begin_inset Formula $az,alt)$
\end_inset

 coordinates.
 Since hour angle and declination are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Earth-based
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 coordinates, this conversion will only depend on the observer's terrestrial
 latitude φ.
 Additionally, we can also convert directly from right ascension and declination
 to azimuth and altitude by multiplying our coordinates by both rotational
 matrices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{(ha,\delta)\rightarrow(az,alt)}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
-\sin(\phi) & 0 & \cos(\phi)\\
0 & -1 & 0\\
\cos(\phi) & 0 & \sin(\phi)
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:HA, Dec to Az, Alt}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Interferometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interferometry is the practice of using two or more radio telescopes in
 a synchronized array to observe astronomical sources and make data measurements.
 A basic interferometer is composed of the array of radio telescopes along
 with the electronics used to measure, record, and detect signals.
 For this lab, my partners and I used a narrow band two-element interferometer
 located on the roof of Kroeber Hall at UC Berkeley, separated along an
 approximately East-West baseline.
 Additionally, the interferometer operates at about 10.67 GHz, the baseline
 separation between the two dishes is approximately B = 10-m, and the observe
 radiation is at a wavelength of λ = 2.5 cm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Two-Element Interferometer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A two-element interferometer (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "two element inter Figure"

\end_inset

) consists of two telescopes separated by a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{b}$
\end_inset

 known as the baseline.
 In this system, both antennas simultaneously receive electromagnetic radiation
 from a specified astronomical source located above in the sky in the direction
 of the unit vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{s}$
\end_inset

.
 Since astronomical sources are extremely far away, radiation is transmitted
 to the antennas in the form of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

plane waves.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 A plane wave is a constant-frequency waveform in which the wavefronts are
 infinite parallel planes of constant peak-to-peak amplitude normal to the
 phase velocity vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ensuremath{\vec{b}}\cdot\hat{s}=\left|\ensuremath{\vec{b}}\right|\cos\theta,\hspace{3em}\tau=\frac{\ensuremath{\vec{b}_{ij}}\cdot\hat{s}}{c}\label{eq:baseline}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /Users/ppkantorski/Documents/Radio_Astronomy/Lab_3/Pictures/two_element_inter.jpg
	scale 75
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
When an incoming radio wave approaches the Earth at an angle, the radio
 wave will arrive at the two separated telescopes at slightly different
 times.
 In this picture, the delay in arrival time, also known as the geometric
 delay, is the distance X divided by the velocity of light.
 Also, the angular resolution of the interferometer is equal to the wavelength
 divided by the effective baseline of the telescopes.
 When the object being observed is directly overhead, the effective baseline
 is approximately equal to the distance between the two telescopes.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "two element inter Figure"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the plane waves from astronomical sources reach the radio dishes, one
 antenna will end up receiving the signal before the other.
 The time that passes between the receiving of the signals is calls the
 geometric delay and is denoted with the variable τ.
 In order to accurately find the value of τ, we must know the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

extra distance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that the plane waves have to travel before reaching the second telescope.
 This distance is approximately equal to the baseline vector dotted with
 the directional unit vector.
 Additionally, since the direction of the source is a unit vector, this
 distance is approximately the projection of the baseline vector onto the
 unit vector (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:baseline"

\end_inset

) with
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 being the angle between the baseline and direction vectors.
 If the baseline projection of the interferometer is known, then the geometric
 delay τ can be calculated by dividing this distance by the velocity of
 the plane waves (the speed of light, c).
 When this delay is an exact multiple of the wavelength, then the waves
 detected by the two telescopes will be in phase and will add together when
 combined.
 If they are not, then they will be out of phase and interfere with one
 another.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If there is another source in the sky at a different direction from the
 first, the second source will also cause a geometric delay between the
 two antennas.
 However, this would be different from the geometric delay of the first
 source because it would have different directional unit vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{s}$
\end_inset

.
 Because each antenna is receiving a signal from each source, what is detected
 is actually the total signal of the two.
 In order to separate the signals from each source at each radio dish, we
 must correlate the total signals with each other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Correlation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Correlation is very similar to convolution theorem.
 However, there is a slight difference.
 When correlating two signals, the Fourier transform of the second function
 is conjugated before being multiplied and integrated over.
 Additionally, by correlating a flat-spectrum signal with a time-shifted
 version of itself, this will yield a measure of the power of the signal
 at the delay corresponding to the time shift.
 For a two-element interferometer, the total signal detected by each antenna
 can be represented as shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:total signal"

\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $e{}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e{}_{2}$
\end_inset

 are the signals given by each source at times t and the difference between
 t and either 
\begin_inset Formula $τ_{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $τ_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(t)=e_{1}(t)+e_{2}(t),\hspace{3em}E_{j}(t)=e_{1}(t-\tau_{1})+e_{2}(t-\tau_{2})\label{eq:total signal}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(f\star g)(\tau)=\int{f(t)g^{*}(t-\tau)}dt\label{eq: correlation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By using 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}(t)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{j}(t)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 in the correlation equation (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq: correlation"

\end_inset

) respectively, the correlation equation then becomes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(f\star g)(\tau)=\int{e_{1}(t)e_{1}(t-\tau_{1}-\tau)+e_{2}(t)e_{2}(t-\tau_{2}-\tau)}dt\label{eq:correlation application 1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to extract meaningful signal information out of the integral shown
 above in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:correlation application 1"

\end_inset

, the two functions must both not average to zero.
 This is only possible when τ is equal either to 
\begin_inset Formula $-τ_{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-τ_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 In the case of τ = 
\begin_inset Formula $-τ_{1}$
\end_inset

, the correlation integral of the two sources becomes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(f\star g)(\tau)=\int e_{1}(t)e_{1}(t-\tau_{1}-(-\tau_{1}))dt=\int e_{1}(t)e_{1}(t)dt=\,<e_{1}^{2}>
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this expression, the 
\begin_inset Formula $e{}_{2}$
\end_inset

 term averages to zero because 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{2}-(-\tau_{1})$
\end_inset

 does not cancel.
 The final result is the average power received by the antennas for either
 the first or second source depending on the geometric delay τ.
 So for a set of different geometric delays, there is a set of power values
 that rises above signals that cannot be correlated.
 The set of power values are essentially noise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Visibility Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A single frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

 with corresponding wavelength λ has a number of wavelengths according to
 the relationship to the baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{ij}\nu=\frac{\vec{b}\cdot\hat{s}}{\lambda}$
\end_inset

.
 By knowing the number of wavelengths between the two antennas, we can say
 that for a signal of a particular frequency coming from a source in direction
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{s}$
\end_inset

, the complex phase difference between that signal measured at antenna i
 and antenna j will be 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\text{θ}}$
\end_inset

, where θ is the angle covered by the wave as it propagates from i to j.
 Since θ is 2π times the number of wavelengths, we can the phase difference
 Δφ (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:phase diff"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta\phi=e^{-2\pi i\tau_{ij}\nu}=e^{-2\pi i\frac{\vec{b}\cdot\hat{s}}{\lambda}}\label{eq:phase diff}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The phase difference Δφ is as shown in the equation above frequency dependent.
 At particular frequencies, Δφ also varies with position across the sky.
 The pattern traced by the complex phase difference on the sky is called
 the “fringe pattern” of the interferometer.
 To extrapolate from a single baseline in a single direction a picture of
 how an entire array might respond to the entire sky that falls within the
 primary beam of the correlated telescopes, we must define coordinates represent
ing the length of a baseline in units of wavelength (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:baseline in wavelength"

\end_inset

).
 Additionally, we must split the source direction vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{s}$
\end_inset

 into its components.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\vec{b}}{\lambda}\equiv(u,v,w),\hspace{3em}\hat{s}\equiv(l,m,\sqrt{1-l^{2}-m^{2}})\label{eq:baseline in wavelength}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:baseline in wavelength"

\end_inset

, u represents the east-west component of the baseline, v the north-south
 component, w the vertical (up-down) component, l the east-west direction
 on the sky, and m the north-south direction.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{1-l^{2}-m^{2}}$
\end_inset

 component of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{s}$
\end_inset

 comes from the fact that we restrict our direction to be a unit vector.
 Using these components, the response of a baseline, or “visibility” V,
 as a function of the u,v,w separation of the antennas, integrated over
 all source intensity I on the sky as a function of l and m can be written
 as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V(u,v)=\int\int{A(l,m)\cdot I(l,m)\cdot e^{-2\pi i(ul+vm+w\sqrt{1-l^{2}-m^{2}})}dl\ dm}\label{eq:Visibility}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Visibility"

\end_inset

 is the complete form of the “visibility equation” and is also sometimes
 referred to as the “measurement equation” of an interferometer.
 Variable A(l,m) is the response of the primary beam of the antennas as
 a function of it's direction on the sky.
 In general, A(l,m) and I(l,m) are grouped together because the sky is always
 perceived through the filter of the primary beam.
 The product between the two is sometimes referred to as the “perceived
 intensity
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the beam.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Visibility"

\end_inset

 can be easier understood if we make a simplifying assumption known as the
 “flat-sky” approximation.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

flat-sky
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 approximation is either that w = 0 or that the primary beam A(l,m) is sufficien
tly small that 
\begin_inset Formula $l,m\ll1$
\end_inset

, making the component
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{1-l^{2}-m^{2}}\approx1$
\end_inset

.
 For either approximation, the response of a baseline does not have to account
 for the fact that the sky is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

curved surface
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of a sphere.
 With this assumption, the 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-2\pi iw\sqrt{1}}$
\end_inset

 term is no longer a function of l and m and can therefore be removed from
 the integral (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:visibility fixed"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V(u,v)=e^{-2\pi iw}\int\int{A(l,m)\cdot I(l,m)\cdot e^{-2\pi i(ul+vm)}dl\ dm}\label{eq:visibility fixed}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This version of the visibility equation shown above states that when the
 telescopes are phased to a “phase center” through a choice of a corresponding
 component 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{\text{−}2\text{π}iw}$
\end_inset

 with w being the baseline component along the direction toward the phase
 center in units of wavelength, the visibility V(u,v) is becomes just the
 Fourier Transform of the perceived sky (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "beam Figure"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

