\begin_layout Standard
To better understand the fringe-pattern of a baseline on the sky, we can
 equivalently think of taking an image of the sky in 
\begin_inset Formula $l,m$
\end_inset

 coordinates and then Fourier transform to create a “uv-plane” with coordinates
 being 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

inverse angles.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Because an inverse angle is the same thing as a wavelength, the uv-plane
 will have coordinates of u,v.
 Furthermore, for each pair of antennas, there will be two samples; one
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $u,v$
\end_inset

 and another at 
\begin_inset Formula $−u,−v$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

