\begin_layout Standard
Since the sky is real-valued, these two Fourier components are related by
 a complex conjugate.
 This means that if we measure V(u,v) at 
\begin_inset Formula $u,v$
\end_inset

, we will also have to measure 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(u,v)$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $−u,−v$
\end_inset

.
 The sampling of the uv-plane is done by multiplying the true uv-plane with
 the sampling pattern computed.
 This result is what we would get if we took visibilities measured from
 an interferometer and then placed each visibility V(u,v) at the corresponding
 
\begin_inset Formula $u,v$
\end_inset

 (along with 
\begin_inset Formula $−u,−v$
\end_inset

) coordinates of a matrix.
 Moreover, if you then take the inverse Fourier Transform of this sampled
 uv-plane, you get a dirty image (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "Dirty Beam Figure-2"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

