\begin_layout Standard
Deconvolution attempts to compensate for the information that is lost by
 sampling part of the uv-plane by inserting prior information about the
 sky.
 This information can be along the lines of just knowing that the sky is
 a set of point-sources to knowing that we want the smoothest sky that fits
 our data to the level of noise.
 Nevertheless, deconvolution is not a well-posed issue until you decide
 precisely what prior information you want to use for the sky.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methods and Observations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Using the Interferometer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this lab, the interferometer used in this lab has a relatively short
 baseline and the fringe spacing is larger than the size of all sources
 except for the Sun and Moon.
 Because of this, all of these sources looked like “point sources” from
 which all the radiation appears to come from a single point like a star
 in optical astronomy.
 The output of our interferometer is a sinusoidal-like signal called the
 “fringe”.
 All information for the data collected using the interferometer is stored
 in the frequency, amplitude, and phase of the fringe.
 Additionally, if we initially know the baseline, the fringe properties
 become indicators of the point-source's declination.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The interferometer we used in this lab is also called a multiplying interferomet
er.
 A multiplying interferometer takes the signals from the two telescopes
 and multiplies them together using a mixer.
 We repetitively measured and recorded the time average over a suitable
 interval.
 Using the cross product between the signals is important because its average
 value is zero unless there if there is a source.
 Thus, any detected signal has to come from the sky instead of from the
 instrument itself.
 When we observe a source, the fringe amplitude has no zero offset and the
 amplitude is therefore directly proportional to the source's brightness
 or flux.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Measuring Accurate Declinations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since our interferometer has a baseline of about 10-m, we can recover a
 fringe spacing of about 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\text{λ}}{B}\sim10\text{′}$
\end_inset

.
 From a horizon-to-horizon measurement, we were also able to measure the
 declination approximately 100 times more accurately.
 Furthermore, we can also achieve partial angular resolution of the Sun
 and Moon along with their diameters to a fraction of a percent.
\end_layout

