\begin_layout Subsection
Object Tracking in Python
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To track astronomical sources with the interferometer using the programming
 language Python, my lab partners and I used the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

PyEphem
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Radiolab
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Python libraries to position our telescope and monitor time.
 By using UTC, we were able to figure out what sidereal time it is in England
 at a longitude of 0º in hours, minutes, and seconds.
 Since right ascension is measured in units of time, this told us what part
 of the sky is overhead at the prime meridian.
 From our current longitude at Berkeley (37.8732º N, -122.2573º), we figured
 out the time offset we had add to get the current local sidereal time.
 The LST tells us what part of the sky is directly overhead at our location.
 By subtracting from the RA the LST of our astronomical sources, we were
 able to get the hour angle as it appears from our location.
 Finally, from using the latitude, HA, and the declination of our sources,
 we were able to figure out precisely how and where to point the telescope.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Point Sources
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While choosing point sources, my partners and I had to be careful.
 Since the fringe frequency is dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $\cosδ$
\end_inset

, this means that we did not measure δ directly, but rather 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos\text{\ensuremath{δ}}$
\end_inset

.
 Southern sources presented a minor problem for horizon-to-horizon tracking
 because of the presence of the Campanile at UC Berkeley.
 To compensate for this, we had to delete some of our data.
 Additionally, our telescope looking for Northern sources may be inaccurate,
 leading to loss of the signal and noise.
 Our observation frequency was also in the TV satellite band.
 Because geostationary TV satellites resides in the southern skies, they
 can generate strong signals that could enter the telescope's dish sidelines
 and produce fringes.
\end_layout

