\begin_layout Subsection
Additional Details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the two-element interferometer telescope has different distances for
 each antenna from the source, it’s convenient to think of the different
 distances in terms of relative path delay in units of time for the two
 telescopes.
 As stated earlier, this is called the geometrical path delay
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{g}$
\end_inset

.
 However, the signals have to travel through a lot of electronics before
 they are finally multiplied.
 Additionally, these two paths are not necessarily of equal length.
 This makes there an additional relative delay from the difference in cable
 length 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{c}$
\end_inset

.
 The total relative delay is the sum of the two (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:relative delay"

\end_inset

).
 While 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{g}$
\end_inset

 is a function of time, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{c}$
\end_inset

 is independent of time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tau_{total}=\tau_{g}(h_{s})+\tau_{c},\hspace{3em}\tau_{g}(h_{s})=\left[\frac{B_{y}}{c}\cos\delta\right]\sin h_{s}\label{eq:relative delay}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The local fringe frequency is the frequency of a certain point on the sky.
 To see this, we can expand the hour angle term 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin h_{s}$
\end_inset

 into a Taylor series centered about the current hour angle of the source
 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{s}$
\end_inset

 (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:expand hour"

\end_inset

).
 The local fringe frequency is contained in the second term of Equation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:expand hour"

\end_inset

 because for a small region around 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{s}$
\end_inset

 in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:fringe amplitude for pt"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(h)$
\end_inset

 varies as 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{f}\text{∆}h$
\end_inset

.
 The local fringe frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{f}$
\end_inset

 is shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:fringe freq"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sin h=\left.\sin h_{s}+\triangle h\frac{d\sin h}{dh}\right|_{h_{s}}=\sin h_{s}+\triangle h\cos h_{s}\label{eq:expand hour}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f_{f}=\frac{C}{2\pi}\cos(h_{s})=\left[\frac{B_{y}}{\lambda}\cos\delta\right]\cos h_{s}\label{eq:fringe freq}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:fringe freq"

\end_inset

 represents the local fringe frequency in cycles per radian on the sky.
 To convert to cycles per hour, we can multiply 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dh_{s}}{dy}=\frac{2\pi}{24}$
\end_inset

 where the number 24 represents the number of hours in a day.
 For instance, at the meridian, assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{y}\approx10m$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $λ=2.5cm$
\end_inset

, the fringe frequency is approximately 
\begin_inset Formula $0.029\cos\text{δ}$
\end_inset

 cycles per second while the period is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{f_{f}}=\frac{35}{\cos\delta}=35s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Least Square Fitting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A least squares method can be used on the filtered data from the point sources
 and from the Sun and Moon using the fringe amplitude (Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:fringe amplitude for pt"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:fringe amplitude for sun/moon"

\end_inset

).
 For instance, for the point sources, this is done by minimizing the square
 of the residuals (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

) for particular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{c}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $B{}_{y}$
\end_inset

.
 Using the following procedure shown below, my lab partners and I were able
 to fit for both 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{c}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $B{}_{y}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(1) Using python, we inserted Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:fringe amplitude for pt"

\end_inset

 with guessed constant coefficients for the
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{c}$
\end_inset

 dependent factors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(2) Next, we iterate over a range of possible baseline values 
\begin_inset Formula $B{}_{y}$
\end_inset

, and apply a least squares fit to each value.
 This provided us with a 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 and values of A and B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(3) By looking at the specific value of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{y}$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the minimum 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}$
\end_inset

 value, this gives us the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

least squares fit
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{y}$
\end_inset

 , 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Measuring The Diameter Of A Circular Source
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this lab, goal was to measure and compare the diameters of the Sun and
 Moon.
 Assuming that the sources are uniformly-bright disks of radius R, this
 makes the intensity distribution approximately equal to Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Intensity distribution circular"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
I(\triangle h)=\frac{(R^{2}-\triangle h^{2})^{1/2}}{R}\label{eq:Intensity distribution circular}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, to obtain the theoretical modulating function 
\begin_inset Formula $MF{}_{theory}$
\end_inset

, we can use the following expression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MF_{theory}=\frac{1}{R}\intop_{-R}^{R}(R^{2}-\Delta h^{2})^{1/2}\cos(2\pi f_{f}\Delta h)d\Delta h\label{eq:modulation function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we want to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $MF{}_{theory}$
\end_inset

 analytically, this can be done by substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(R\cos\theta)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta h$
\end_inset

.
 The result is a Bessel function.
 To do this, we can split 
\begin_inset Formula $I(\Delta h)$
\end_inset

 into 2N+1 tiny slices.
 Since the total number is odd, this makes the slices symmetric about 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta h=0$
\end_inset

.
 Each of these slices has width 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta h=\frac{R}{N}$
\end_inset

 along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta h_{n}=n\delta h$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 ranges from 
\begin_inset Formula $-N$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $+N$
\end_inset

.
 After being rewritten, the integral becomes the sum shown below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MF_{theory}\approx\delta h\sum_{n=-N}^{n=+N}\left[1-(\frac{n}{N})^{2}\right]^{1/2}\cos(\frac{2\pi f_{f}Rn}{N})\label{eq:mod funct final}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Data Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Python Coding to Clean Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To treat the data my partners and I gathered, I made a Python script to
 eliminate bad data points for all my data, normalize the plot to the origin,
 fit an envelope for the Sun and Moon data, plot the power spectrum, and
 perform a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Least Square Mean
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 curve fit to return the baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{y}$
\end_inset

 for point sources, and use polynomial curve fitting for the a parabolic
 portion of the Sun and Moon to derive a Bessel function, and calculate
 the angular diameter of the sources.
 Additionally, my script was also built to make plots for just about any
 datasets taken from the interferometer given a specified file path.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To normalize datasets to the x-axis, I using a method of data smoothing
 called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Boxcar Averaging
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This was done by averaging 100 points above and below each datapoint to
 create an array of average data.
 Initially, I used the Boxcar Average to find data points to cut out of
 the raw data before entering the Boxcar average.
 Then, by subtracting out the average data plot from the original graph,
 the data becomes centered about roughly 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

y = 0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

cleaning
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 my data data calculations and analysis possible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next thing I did was fit a curve to the envelope of the Sun and Moon's
 normalized data.
 To find the envelop, I cut out from the data points points whose derivative
 was greater than a number relatively close to 0.
 This allowed me to only plot points along the peaks in the signal.
 Then, by taking the absolute value of the truncated data and using another
 Boxcar average on the data points to smooth the data, I was able to get
 a nice envelope for the data.
 For the Moon, however, because the data points had a lot more spikes, I
 had to use a scaling factor of about 
\begin_inset Formula $2.7$
\end_inset

 to scale my envelop to the correct amplitude.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After finding the envelop curve for my Sun and Moon data, I was then able
 to fit a parabolic polynomial curve to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

parabola like
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 region of my data.
 By using modulation function shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:mod funct final"

\end_inset

 along with the constants taken from the fitted polynomials, I was then
 able to derive a Bessel function to approximate the diameter of the Sun
 and the Moon.
\end_layout

