\begin_layout Standard
The average baseline was approximately 10.0637-m, a value within 0.6365% of
 the initial 10-m guess.
 Errors in data result may have resulted from bad data measurements, possible
 (but not likely) flawes in coding, external signal noise from the location
 of the telescopes, and/or improper selection of data for the least square
 fitting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interferometry is an extremely useful tool for making more precise measurements
 than from using single radio dishes.
 By synchronizing two radio telescopes separated by a baseline, this effectively
 increases the resolution of data measurements.
 Since there is a baseline separation when using the interferometer, there
 is a distinct geometric delay between the two dishes.
 This geometric delay is what produces the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fringe patterns
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 we observed.
 Fringe patterns can be used to determine either the baseline of the interferome
ters or declination of an astronomical point source being tracked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When measuring the Sun or the Moon with the interferometer, the size of
 the source is relatively larger than the interferometer we used.
 Because of this, this gives rise to a modulating function.
 From the modulating function, the angular radius can then be determined
 from the function's shape.
 Although our data could have been better, results were still fairly close
 to what we expected.
 Ultimately, this lab has taught how to properly analyze singals and data
 in Python, use an interferometer to make measurements, and aproximate the
 baseline from point sources and angular diameter for the Sun and Moon.
 
\end_layout

